2026-06-30 15:22:33,582 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5a8b5e61-f7eb-41fd-bdb1-8e0cf22a78ff', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nQE04236-2.raw\nQE04236.raw\nQE04237-2.raw\nQE04237.raw\nQE04238-2.raw\nQE04238.raw\nQE04239-2.raw\nQE04239.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCRISPR and biochemical screens identify MAZ as a cofactor in CTCF-mediated insulation at Hox clusters\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCCCTC-binding factor (CTCF) is critical to three-dimensional genome organization. Upon differentiation, CTCF insulates active and repressed genes within Hox gene clusters. We conducted a genome-wide CRISPR knockout (KO) screen to identify genes required for CTCF-boundary activity at the HoxA cluster, complemented by biochemical approaches. Among the candidates, we identified Myc-associated zinc-finger protein (MAZ) as a cofactor in CTCF insulation. MAZ colocalizes with CTCF at chromatin borders and, similar to CTCF, interacts with the cohesin subunit RAD21. MAZ KO disrupts gene expression and local contacts within topologically associating domains. Similar to CTCF motif deletions, MAZ motif deletions lead to derepression of posterior Hox genes immediately after CTCF boundaries upon differentiation, giving rise to homeotic transformations in mouse. Thus, MAZ is a factor contributing to appropriate insulation, gene expression and genomic architecture during development.\nGenome-wide screens identify several genes, including MAZ, required for CTCF-mediated insulation. MAZ interacts with cohesin, and MAZ motif deletions derepress posterior Hox gene expression, leading to homeotic transformations in mouse.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nThis study was performed under compliance with ethical regulations and approved by New York University (NYU)/NYU Grossman School of Medicine’s Institutional Biosafety Committee.\nCell culture and MN differentiation\nE14TG2a \ufeffmouse ESCs (ES-E14TG2a,\ufeff ATCC, CRL-1821) were cultured in standard medium supplemented with LIF and 2i conditions (1\u2009mM MEK1/2 inhibitor (PD0325901, Stemgent) and 3\u2009mM GSK3 inhibitor (CHIR99021, Stemgent)). For MN differentiation, a previously described protocol was applied. Briefly, ESCs were differentiated into embryoid bodies in 2 days, and further patterning was induced with addition of 1\u2009μM all-trans-retinoic acid (Sigma) and 0.5\u2009μM smoothened agonist (Calbiochem). Biological replicates stand for independent differentiation experiments performed. 293FT cells (R70007, Thermo Fisher Scientific) were cultured in standard medium as described in the manufacturer’s protocol.\nCRISPR genome editing\nsgRNAs were designed using CRISPR design tools (http://crispr.mit.edu/; currently available at https://benchling.com). All sgRNAs were cloned into SpCas9-2AGFP vector (Addgene: PX458) or into a lentiviral vector lentiGuide-puro (52963, Addgene). The sgRNAs were transfected into ESCs using Lipofectamine 2000 (Invitrogen) as described previously or infected into an ESC clone expressing lentiCas9-blast (52962, Addgene). In the case of CRISPR knockin cell lines, donor DNA (1 μl of 10 μM single-stranded DNA oligonucleotide or 3\u2009μg pBluescriptSK (+) plasmid containing donor DNA) were transfected with 1\u2009μg px458-sgRNAs. Single clones from GFP-positive FACS-sorted cells or puromycin (InvivoGen)-resistant cells were genotyped and confirmed by sequencing. When necessary, PCR products were further assessed by TOPO cloning (Invitrogen) and sequencing to distinguish the amplified products of different alleles. The sequencing chromatograms were aligned in Benchling. All sgRNAs, donors and genotyping primers are shown in Supplementary Table 1.\nCell line generation for Hoxa5:a7 dual reporter in WT and CTCF (Δ5|6:6|7) backgrounds\nTo generate Hoxa5:a7 dual-reporter cells, ESCs were sequentially targeted at Hoxa5 and Hoxa7 loci, respectively. ESCs were initially transfected with sgRNA and donor pBluescriptSK (+) plasmid for Hoxa5-P2A-mCherry cell line generation using Lipofectamine (Invitrogen). Hoxa5-mCherry cell line was confirmed through genotyping, sequencing, and FACS analysis upon MN differentiation for the homozygous insertion of reporter. Next, the Hoxa5-mCherry cell line was transfected with sgRNA and donor pBluescriptSK (+) plasmid for generation of the dual Hoxa5:a7 knock-in cell line, which was confirmed by genotyping, sequencing, and FACS analysis for the homozygous insertion of reporter. To demonstrate Hoxa7-P2A-eGFP expression in MNs, CTCF binding sites at Hoxa5|6 and Hoxa6|7 were removed via sequential CRISPR genome editing using respective sgRNAs, generating CTCF (Δ5|6) and CTCF (Δ5|6:6|7) deletion lines in the Hoxa5:a7 dual-reporter background. For CRISPR library screen experiments, WT or CTCF (Δ5|6:6|7) dual-reporter lines were transduced with lentiCas9-blast (Addgene, 52962), and Cas9-expressing clones were obtained after selection with blasticidin (InvivoGen).\nCell line generation for FLAG-CTCF-tagged cells\nTo generate the CTCF C-terminal FLAG-tagged cell line, E14TG2a mouse ESCs were targeted with sgRNA in SpCas9-2AGFP vector (Addgene, PX458) and single-stranded donor oligonucleotide at the Ctcf locus. The cell line was confirmed by genotyping, sequencing, and western blot for FLAG-CTCF.\nCell line generation for MAZ KO cells\nWT or CTCF (Δ5|6:6|7) Hoxa5:a7 dual-reporter cells expressing Cas9 were targeted with sgRNAs in lentiGuide-puro vector for Maz. Knock-out of Maz was confirmed by genotyping, sequencing, and western blot.\nCell line generation for MAZ binding site deletions\nHoxa5:a7 dual-reporter cells were targeted with sgRNAs in SpCas9-2AGFP vector (Addgene, PX458) for MAZ binding sites at HoxA, HoxD or HoxC clusters. Specific MAZ binding site deletions were confirmed by genotyping and sequencing.\nCRISPR screens\nCRISPR genome-wide screens were done using methods described previously. Briefly, GeCKO genome-wide pooled CRISPR libraries (Addgene, 1000000053) were amplified and deep-sequenced to confirm sgRNA representations, as shown previously. A Cas9-expressing Hoxa5:a7 ESC clone was transduced with the pooled lentiviral sgRNAs at a low multiplicity of infection (~0.4). The reporter ESCs were selected with puromycin, cultured for 7 days, differentiated into MNs in 6 days and sorted by FACS into two MN populations: (1) WT MNs (mCherry-positive/eGFP-negative cells) and (2) CTCF-boundary-disrupted MNs (double-positive cells). During the screens, 300× and 1,000× coverage was applied for genome-wide screens and secondary screens, respectively. CRISPR libraries were prepared at each time point and/or sorted population, and the relative sgRNA representation was assessed using next-generation sequencing, as described previously.\nCustom library construction for secondary CRISPR screens\nsgRNAs for custom library used in the secondary CRISPR screens were retrieved from a previously designed genome-wide mouse CRISPR KO pooled library (Brie). When required for several genes, sgRNAs were designed by using the Broad Institute CRISPRko gRNA design tools (https://portals.broadinstitute.org/gpp/public/analysis-tools/sgrna-design). All sgRNAs in the custom library in Supplementary Dataset 6 were synthesized as a pool by Twist Bioscience. The custom library was cloned into lentiGuide-puro vector, amplified and verified in terms of representation of all constructs using methods described previously.\nFlow cytometry\nCells were trypsinized, filtered and stained with 4,6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (Sigma) to eliminate dead cells during analysis of Hoxa5:a7 reporters in ESCs and MNs. Hoxa5:a7 dual fluorescent reporter cells in WT versus other backgrounds were assessed by using single-color fluorescent reporters as controls in the same cell type as analyzed (i.e., MNs). Hb9-T2A-eGFP reporter cells (not shown) were used as GFP control in MNs (Supplementary Fig. 7a). For cell cycle analysis, ESCs were fixed in 75% ethanol, and DNA was stained with propidium iodide (Thermo Fisher Scientific) after RNase A (Thermo Fisher Scientific) treatment. FlowJo (version 8.7) was used for all FACS analysis (Supplementary Fig. 7b).\nExpression analysis\nRNA was purified from cells with RNAeasy Plus Mini kit (Qiagen), and RT was performed on 1 μg RNA by using Superscript III (Life Technologies) and random hexamers (Thermo Fisher Scientific). RT-qPCRs were performed in replicates on 100\u2009ng cDNA using PowerUp SYBR Green Master Mix (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The primers are listed in Supplementary Table 2. For RNA-seq analysis, 1\u2009μg RNA was used to prepare ribo-minus RNA-seq libraries according to the manufacturer’s protocols by the NYU Genome Technology Center.\nChIP-seq\nChIP-seq experiments were performed as described previously (see details regarding ESC fixation in Oksuz et al. and MN fixation in Narendra et al.). Briefly, cells were fixed with 1% formaldehyde, nuclei were isolated and chromatin was fragmented to ~250\u2009bp using a Diagenode Bioruptor. ChIP was performed using antibodies listed in Supplementary Table 2. Chromatin from Drosophila (1:100 ratio to ESC- or MN-derived chromatin), and Drosophila-specific H2Av antibody was used as a spike-in control in each sample. For ChIP-seq, libraries were prepared as described previously using 1–30\u2009ng immunoprecipitated DNA. ChIP-qPCRs were performed with PowerUp SYBR Green Master Mix (Thermo Fisher Scientific) and detected by the Stratagene Mx3005p or QuantStudio 5 (Thermo Fisher Scientific) instrument. All ChIP-qPCR primers are listed in Supplementary Table 2.\nCUT&RUN\nThis method was performed as described previously using 100\u2009000–200\u2009000 cells that were sorted for double-positive (Hoxa5-P2A-mCherry and Hoxa7-P2A-eGFP) populations. WT MNs were treated similarly and collected through FACS. The cells were re-counted after sorting and the published protocol detailed in https://www.protocols.io by Janssens and Henikoff was followed. CUT&RUN experiments were analyzed with the methods described for ChIP-seq below.\nPreparation of 4C template\nCells were processed for 4C sequencing (4C-seq) as described previously. Cells were trypsinized and counted, and 1 × 107 cells were crosslinked with the crosslinking solution (2% formaldehyde and 10% FBS in 1× PBS) for 10\u2009min at room temperature. After the reaction was quenched with glycine, cells were lysed on ice with 1\u2009ml lys\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nMus musculus (mouse)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
